//   u7-r-premed-time  how brief premeditation may be. Keyed "majority" = no appreciable time (Commonwealth v. Carroll, 412 Pa.
//                     525 (1963): "no time is too short") and "minority" = appreciable reflection shown by planning, motive or
//                     manner (People v. Anderson, Cal. 1968; State v. Guthrie, W. Va. 1995). Confirm which side is the majority
//                     in LaFave, Substantive Criminal Law, or a 50-state survey (crim.md §16.5).
//   u7-r-imperfect    imperfect self-defense: now keyed "traditional" (no mitigation) vs "modern" (mitigates to voluntary
//                     manslaughter) with no count of jurisdictions, plus the aggressor variant and the MPC 3.09(2) note in the
//                     exceptions. Check against LaFave (self-defense and manslaughter chapters) or a 50-state survey.
//   u7-h-imperfect    the same doctrine (rule stated in the stem); the NCBE outline does not name it, so it is low-yield.
// Upgraded 2026-11-02: u7-r-kidnap to "general" (the MPC 212.1 paraphrase matches the Code's structure: removal from residence or
//   business or a substantial distance, or confinement for a substantial period in a place of isolation, with a listed purpose;
//   the traditional/modern labels on incidental movement match standard sources). u7-c-durham is "general" (Brawner, 1972).
//
